A||||36||84sleepr||Engine Swap????||||1||
Z||000000||84sleepr||12-16-2001||06:09 PM||||Well, I had a plan for a 3800SC but after doing some reading I'm starting to quesion my choice.  Hers my story: I started off wanting a 3.8SC and was able to track one down through a friend, my only problem is I just don't like messing with wiring and fuel injection.  I've always been a huge fan of carburation, It could be cus I miss my old camaro with the 350.  I vowed that I'd get another camaro but since then I picked up my first fiero and i've been in love with them.  I'm starting to figure maybe I should combine my 2 passions: fieros and sbc and go for the 350 swap, carburated with a slightly lumpy cam.  Price wise, after looking at archie's at a kit this seems like a very good possibility... so should i go with a 3800sc or a 350?  I'm kinda stuck on this one, what's the best value? easiest? I would think a 350...<P>One more thing, sorry about the long post, but I've read about some guy using SHO yamaha engine, any info on this.  Cus to make my decision even tougher, I can buy my dad's 1990 Taurus SHO very cheap...for an engine swap of course ...||||reg||1
Z||000001||GTDude||12-16-2001||09:59 PM||||Forget the carbureted engine.  The 3800sc is very very reliable and will give you awesome power. Plus it's a good looking engine.  The regular 3800 would be easier but you'd loose 35 hp.  Carbureted engines are a thing of the past...why spend all that money to do an engine swap and end up with an antiquated engine.  BTW please don't put a ford engine in a fiero.....it's blasphemy!  Good luck and make the right decision. &lt;smile&gt;<P>Phil Pettus<P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||4
Z||000002||fieroX||12-17-2001||12:59 AM||||Go with the 3800 sc.  Why would you want a carbureted V8 that is going to get you 9 miles a gallon and run low 13's when you could have you a supercharged V6 that gets 30 and runs 12's?  Its not that much more difficult to install the L67, and would probably cost about the same after the sbc install kit.  For more info on this swap check in with my website over the next few months I will be detailing this swap to the T.  Without "install" kits or 5 figure amounts.<P>------------------<BR>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fierox.com/logo2small.gif">  </A><BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroX.com</A><BR>coming soon L67 powered 87 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000003||West Coast Fiero||12-17-2001||01:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroX:<BR><B>Go with the 3800 sc.  Why would you want a carbureted V8 that is going to get you 9 miles a gallon and run low 13's when you could have you a supercharged V6 that gets 30 and runs 12's?  Its not that much more difficult to install the L67, and would probably cost about the same after the sbc install kit.  For more info on this swap check in with my website over the next few months I will be detailing this swap to the T.  Without "install" kits or 5 figure amounts.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>this should be interesting  <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/lolup.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000004||ejayerik||12-17-2001||01:14 AM||||Oh great, now I'll never sell my carb.<BR>Thanks guys....lol||||reg||8
Z||000005||fieroX||12-17-2001||02:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B> this should be interesting   <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/lolup.gif">  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Yes it will  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||AgaricX||12-17-2001||03:46 AM||||Well, I've never HAD a carbureted car, so I have no basis for comparison.  I DO however LOVE my 3800SC.  Put a 3.5" pulley on it and you're pushing 275HP and great fuel economy.  I had a 96 Nissan 300Z before and I like driving my Fiero quite a bit more.  (Though I still miss my baby Z)<P>Theres PLENTY of info for doing a 3800 swap too.  I enjoyed it so much... I'm doing a N* next.  When time, money, and wife permit...<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.agaric.net/fiero/AgaricX.jpg">  <BR>'85 SE 3800SC 5 Spd<BR>I WANT A FASTBACK!!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||BOILERMAKER||12-17-2001||06:28 AM||||What's so funny, Eric?  I didn't use a kit and mine is running just fine.||||reg||5
Z||000008||FieroRacer||12-17-2001||11:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AgaricX:<BR><B>Well, I've never HAD a carbureted car, so I have no basis for comparison.  I DO however LOVE my 3800SC.  Put a 3.5" pulley on it and you're pushing 275HP and great fuel economy.  I had a 96 Nissan 300Z before and I like driving my Fiero quite a bit more.  (Though I still miss my baby Z)<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You're not missing much.  My experience with carbs, aaaaarrrrrggggg.  I stomp the gas, about a second later it gets the clue!!  Fuel economy, I've put the car to sit cause the fuel costs aren't worth it.  I will never go back to carbs!!  TBI was a little better, but it wasn't my style.  MPFI is where it's at!!!||||reg||1
Z||000009||Formula88||12-17-2001||11:29 AM||||Carbs are just fine - good performance and economy, IF you know how to work on them.  Like anything mechanical on a car, if you don't know how to work on it or adjust it, you're not gonna get anything out of it.  Now, FI will give better mileage and driveability - it's just more finely tunable than a carb.  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||12-17-2001||12:34 PM||||Sleepr: Didn't you know? There are PLENTY of 'peggers here with no lives that can help you with the wiring... er... well... *I* have no life, and can help with the wiring  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The 3800SC is a wicked-bad motor for this car! If you REALLY insist, and do have the money, don't go for anything less than big v-8 grunt... but a 3800sc will run VERY nicely for you...||||reg||12
Z||000011||fieroX||12-17-2001||01:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BOILERMAKER:<BR><B>What's so funny, Eric?  I didn't use a kit and mine is running just fine.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>He laughs at me now, but just wait until spring.||||reg||1
Z||000012||BOILERMAKER||12-17-2001||03:59 PM||||Yeah, in Spring, I should have my second one done.||||reg||1
Z||000013||West Coast Fiero||12-17-2001||04:46 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BOILERMAKER:<BR><B>What's so funny, Eric?  I didn't use a kit and mine is running just fine.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>not laughing in sarcasim, in fact I <B>FULLY</B> stand behind you in your swap and <FONT COLOR="red"><B>WILL</B></FONT> help you in any way we can with your conversion. My standpoint when I made that comment was that the average Fiero owner does not have access to fabrication and installation equipment, electrical and mechanical schematics, custom driveline fabricators, and all neccessary details to do a full turn key installation by them selves. <P> I'm just curious how your could do this, on an <FONT COLOR="red"><B>AVERAGE HOME MECHANIC</B></FONT>basis and keep it under $5,000<P> keep us all posted<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif">  <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<p>[This message has been edited by West Coast Fiero (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||Butter||12-17-2001||04:49 PM||||84sleepr,<BR>I run a v8 that is carburated.  Right now I get about 14-16mpg with a stock cam in a bored 283cid block.<P>Last engine was a 305 v8 with a split duration high performance cam.  It got 16-18mph with the same intake and carb as above.  <B>Be knowin'</B> I like performance, I'm LEAD Footed and fuel milage isn't a big concern with me.<P>Now I realize that a v8 can be out run by a 3800 but, with all mods to the engines done on the same order I will <B>STOMP A 3800 engine in the ground and beat it to Death.</B>  Thus the old saying <B>"There is no replacement for displacement."</B>  Also when "beefing up" and engine, the SBC has a great record of accepting many mods, as is, with durability.  Do a study and see how much money it takes to produce a 400+ hp 3800 engine vs. a 350 SBC.  Then ask yourself which will hold up better.<P>Carburation vs. Fuel Injection, well that can be changed on either engine.  FI gets better fuel milage and carbs will accept more radical mods with less $$ involved at the sacrifice of economy.<P>This is all my IMHO and is backed up by many that feel as I do, so Flame away you 3800 owners.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/buttersig_1.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Butter (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000015||BOILERMAKER||12-17-2001||05:16 PM||||"STOMP A 3800 engine in the ground and beat it to Death"<P>Uhhhhh, OK, Whatever you say.  I'd love to see that.<P>You can have as much displacement as you want, but if you can't get the tires to stick, you can't do much.||||reg||1
Z||000016||West Coast Fiero||12-17-2001||05:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BOILERMAKER:<BR>[B but if you can't get the tires to stick, you can't do much.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><FONT COLOR="red"><B>MY GOD....</B></FONT>someone also sees the light    <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/up.gif"> <P>essentially, the power to weight ratio is the key, and so is "hook-up" on take off<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000017||jelly2m8||12-17-2001||06:07 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroX:<BR><B>Go with the 3800 sc.  Why would you want a carbureted V8 that is going to get you 9 miles a gallon and run low 13's when you could have you a supercharged V6 that gets 30 and runs 12's? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> On a test this summer with my TPI 5.7L 5 spd Isuzu Fiero it achieved ~35mpg.<P> That was driving the speed limit, no hard acceleration. So as a daily driver, the gas mileage is quite good with the car.<P> Can you say 7% throttle at 55 mph with the 3800? I think not!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by jelly2m8 (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||fieroX||12-17-2001||06:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Butter:<BR><B>  Do a study and see how much money it takes to produce a 400+ hp 3800 engine vs. a 350 SBC.  Then ask yourself which will hold up better.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You are right, you can build a sbc up to 400 hp cheaper than a 3800 sc, but when you do have a 400 hp small block, what kind of mileage do you get then?  and you dare to question the reliability of the 3800 in your last sentence of the quote.  As for "which will hold up better" 3800 any day, by far over the sbc.<BR>This folks is why we need to have the fiero power fest.  ||||reg||1
Z||000019||fieroX||12-17-2001||06:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jelly2m8:<BR><B>  Can you say 7% throttle at 55 mph with the 3800? I think not!<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Yeah probably around in there somewhere.  4th gear, locked up, probably 12% @ 70mph.  I'll check it out on my friends autotap.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||jelly2m8||12-17-2001||07:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroX:<BR><B> <BR>Yeah probably around in there somewhere.  4th gear, locked up, probably 12% @ 70mph.  I'll check it out on my friends autotap.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Cool, but you see that driving a V8 sensibly, there is potential for good gas mileage too.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||MoneypitGT||12-17-2001||09:00 PM||||Then there is also the question of transmissions.....300hp sb with stock fiero tranny with a clutch that doesn't quite grab or a modified auto with the 3800sc that you can add a slip diff. and a high stall converter to and beat the piss out of. Check out Thrasers 12sec, stock internals, modified tranny, daily driven boat of a car: <IMG SRC="http://www.c-ya-racing.net/db3/00266/c-ya-racing.net/_uimages/kcburnout3small.JPG">  I would stick with a 3800.<P>------------------<BR>  <B>Check out my site at:</B>   <A HREF="http://www.customdynamix.com" TARGET=_blank>www.customdynamix.com</A> <BR>Turbo Q4 GT Project||||reg||14
Z||000022||jelly2m8||12-17-2001||09:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MoneypitGT:<BR><B>Then there is also the question of transmissions.....300hp sb with stock fiero tranny with a clutch that doesn't quite grab or a modified auto with the 3800sc that you can add a slip diff. and a high stall converter to and beat the piss out of. Check out Thrasers 12sec, stock internals, modified tranny, daily driven boat of a car:  <IMG SRC="http://www.c-ya-racing.net/db3/00266/c-ya-racing.net/_uimages/kcburnout3small.JPG">   I would stick with a 3800.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>nice pic.<P> FWD? I've seen guys do that with a Geo metro!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000023||cadero2dmax||12-17-2001||09:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ejayerik:<BR><B>Oh great, now I'll never sell my carb.<BR>Thanks guys....lol</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I still have some cash to spend, but you haven't told me how much it will take. I want to use it on the Fiero I am building with the 4.9 for the Grassroots Motorsports Challenge - - <P>------------------<BR>George Ryan<BR>A pair of 4.9 Cadero's<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to: <BR>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821 <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)||||reg||1
Z||000024||West Coast Fiero||12-17-2001||10:02 PM||||is everybody <I>that</I> fearfull of fuel injection?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000025||cadero2dmax||12-17-2001||10:16 PM||||Not fearful of injection at all. My daily driver SE 4.9 has injection. My Dodge 24V Turbo Diesel tow rig has injection. My wif's Diamante has injection. <P>But because of the limited funds allowed to build the Challenge car, and the fact the engine I got for $10 has no injection plumbing or TB - -  I am carburating it. I am using the stock Caddy manifold, with an adapter and the carb feeding it. <P>I currently have a stock 2bbl from an early 350 (about 325CFM), but could use a little more flow. Thus my interest in the carb of ejayerik.<BR> <BR>G<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000026||West Coast Fiero||12-17-2001||10:20 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadero2dmax:<BR><B>Not fearful of injection at all. My daily driver SE 4.9 has injection. My Dodge 24V Turbo Diesel tow rig has injection. My wif's Diamante has injection. <P>But because of the limited funds allowed to build the Challenge car, and the fact the engine I got for $10 has no injection plumbing or TB - -  I am carburating it. I am using the stock Caddy manifold, with an adapter and the carb feeding it. <P>I currently have a stock 2bbl from an early 350 (about 325CFM), but could use a little more flow. Thus my interest in the carb of ejayerik.<BR> <BR>G</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><B><FONT COLOR="red">PERFECT</FONT></B> - then make it happen, i'd like to see this cause it sounds pretty sweet<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://clubs.hemmings.com/fieroswest/WestCoast300.gif"> <BR>Eric Nelson<BR>Technician,<BR>West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000027||Hartz||12-17-2001||10:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>is everybody <I>that</I> fearfull of fuel injection?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Not at all.  I love fuel injected V8's in Fieros!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>More seriously, (and less inflammitory!) to answer the original question by 84Sleeper - as you can probably tell by now, there are a variety of opinions on this, and getting a consensus answer on this forum will never happen.  Read read read.  Talk to folks.  Ask them what made them decide one way or another.  Ultimately realize you can't go wrong either way.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>One thing you didn't mention is how you plan to use this car.  Is this going to be a sunny day toy?  A show car?  An autocrosser?  A drag car?  Something to go out and taunt Vettes with?  As with any good project, begin with the end in mind.  Decide how you will use this car, and that will help set priorities - cost, MPG, power, etc.<P>My unsolicited opinion of the things that kill would be swappers, in no particular order:<P>1)  Unrealistic cost expectations.  If I had a nickle for every post that started off with "I want a V8 but Archie's basic economy kit is too much money..."   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> To do a job right will cost many thousands of dollars.  Archie had a post where he was building a car for $4000.  While there are resourceful people out there who have done SBC's for less, for the average swapper, it's going to cost AT LEAST this much.  If you can't afford this much, think about either a different swap, like a 4.x Caddy motor, or wait.<P>2)  Trying to do too much at once.  How many folks try to start with a 400 HP mouse motor with a 150 shot of nitrous?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  Why not try a nice 300 HP motor, and once you get everything sorted, and enjoy driving the car for a while, then perhaps upgrade?<P>3)  Unrealistic goals.  Again, how many posts combine elements of 1 and 2 above, and then state that they want 100% reliability, because it's their daily driver, and oh, yeah, it has to get 30 MPG and run 11's.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Have fun.  Enjoy.  Good luck!<P>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<BR>hartzfeld.home.mindspring.com<P>||||reg||1
Z||000028||FieroMaster88||12-17-2001||11:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>is everybody <I>that</I> fearfull of fuel injection?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Same thing I have wondered!  To each is own I guess.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieromaster88sig.gif">  <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000029||84sleepr||12-18-2001||01:01 AM||||Thanks for all the responses guys.  I'm kinda leaning towards the 350.  I just picture myself pulling into pony coral (the peggers will know about this) with a lumpy V8.  My 1st passion was late 70s camaros with big HP, Now that I've seen the light and discovered fieros I'm wanting to combine the 2....<P>then again...a 3800SC would be sooooo cool!!!<P>Well, i'll have to talk to the winnipeg boys about getting a little bit of a hand either way... I plan on doing this swap in a garage, however I do have access to a lot of tools and such since I do work at a GM dealership...<P>Oh yeah, I believe cars are ment to be driven, so I'd like something I could take to the grocery store, or drive out to an rice meet and kick some import ass with!<P>Thanks again for the feed back!!||||reg||1
Z||000030||fieroX||12-18-2001||02:24 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Hartz:<BR><B><P>3)  Unrealistic goals.  Again, how many posts combine elements of 1 and 2 above, and then state that they want 100% reliability, because it's their daily driver, and oh, yeah, it has to get 30 MPG and run 11's.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR> <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Just keep an eye on <A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroX.com</A>  it will happen please doubt me, It makes for more shock factor when its done.  So Im going to become a forum recluse for awhile, see yall on the flipside.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000031||dourain86gt||12-18-2001||02:24 AM||||Hey guys I know this would be more expensive... but how about a the new Northstar V6 3500 engine? Does anybody have info on that?Would it be much harder to install in a fiero the the 3400 engine? This engine I think is amazing! All Aluminum!!!!!<P>Keep it light, like the fiero was ment to be!||||reg||1
Z||000032||Rare87GT||12-18-2001||03:32 AM||||I don't understand why everyone bashes each other over engine swaps.  Everyone does their own thing and wants to build and swap a certain engine for a reason.  Who cares if someone puts a V6 over a V8 or the other way around.  And if a V6 beats a V8 then so be it.  I wish everyone would give each other praise for trying to do something different, instead we as forum people doubt and think everyone is full of it.  And yes the 3800 SeriesII Supercharged engine that fieroX is swapping will be one heck of a machine. <P>------------------<BR>1987 GT (Maroon)*5 Speed*<BR>(Canadian Car)<BR>2.8L Mods--No Cat, K&N||||reg||1
Z||000033||Hartz||12-18-2001||09:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fieroX:<BR><B> Just keep an eye on <A HREF="http://www.fieroX.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieroX.com</A>  it will happen please doubt me, It makes for more shock factor when its done.  So Im going to become a forum recluse for awhile, see yall on the flipside.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>FieroX - I'm not saying that it can't happen.  In fact, I think the 3800 would make a neat platform for trying this, and I look forward to seeing your efforts.  However, I think for the average guy thinking about starting an engine swap, trying to come up with a combination that will do EVERYTHING well, within a reasonable budget, is going to be difficult.<P>Hartz<P>||||reg||1
Z||000034||fieroX||12-18-2001||09:51 AM||||Im not an average guy.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000035||Hartz||12-18-2001||10:04 AM||||And there you have it!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Like I said, FieroX, I am looking forward to watching your progress - it sounds like a neat project.  My above comments were certainly not directed towards someone who has deep automotive fabrication skills, or has done other swaps in the past.  It's the "Yeah, my buddy has a '72 Chevy truck with a 350 in it, and I've got a small box of tools here and $500 burning a hole in my pocket, how can I make a 400 HP Fiero?" guys that drive me crazy...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Hartz||||reg||10
Z||000036||fieroX||12-18-2001||01:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Hartz:<BR><B>It's the "Yeah, my buddy has a '72 Chevy truck with a 350 in it, and I've got a small box of tools here and $500 burning a hole in my pocket, how can I make a 400 HP Fiero?" guys that drive me crazy...    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Hartz</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I hear you on that one.  These guys are crazy, I mean theyre looking at least $600.  $500 man what are they thinking.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||1
